1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,100
Why do traffic jams appear out of nowhere?

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:05,900
You crawl along, then the road clears.

3
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,620
No crash, no roadworks, nothing.

4
00:00:08,843 --> 00:00:12,380
Here's the surprise: enough cars, close together,

5
00:00:12,310 --> 00:00:14,380
make the jam themselves.

6
00:00:14,367 --> 00:00:17,580
In two thousand eight, Japanese scientists tested it.

7
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:21,660
Twenty-two cars, on a ring two hundred and thirty metres around.

8
00:00:21,590 --> 00:00:24,620
Everyone was told to hold thirty kilometres an hour.

9
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:27,020
No bottleneck, nothing in the way.

10
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:29,980
Within minutes, cars were stopping dead.

11
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:33,260
Here's why. One driver eases off a little.

12
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:36,420
The drivers behind react late and brake harder,

13
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:40,540
so it grows, car after car, until somebody stops.

14
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:43,420
Now watch the jam. The cars go forwards,

15
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:47,740
but the jam slides backwards, at about twenty kilometres an hour.

16
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:52,780
That's a stop-and-go wave. Real highway jams move at that same speed.

17
00:00:52,710 --> 00:00:55,580
How do you stop one? Don't tailgate.

18
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:58,460
Leave a big gap, and keep a steady speed.

19
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:02,940
When the car ahead brakes, don't copy it. Let the gap shrink.

20
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:05,860
You soak up the wave instead of passing it on.

21
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:09,447
Later, one car with a computer on the pedals did just that,

22
00:01:09,323 --> 00:01:10,940
and the waves faded.

23
00:01:10,927 --> 00:01:14,820
So a jam out of nowhere starts with one tap on the brakes.

24
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:16,620
And it can end with you.
